      <h1>Battery Trade &amp; HS Codes</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Market Intelligence · Last reviewed 2026-09-15</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Battery trade is classified under <strong>HS 8507 (electric accumulators)</strong>,
        with subheadings that split by chemistry: <strong>8507.10</strong> for lead-acid starter batteries,
        <strong>8507.20</strong> for other lead-acid, and <strong>8507.60</strong> for lithium-ion. The code determines
        tariffs and how trade statistics are counted, which is why any export or import analysis must state the
        code.
      </div>

      <h2>The main HS codes</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>HS code</th><th>Category</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>8507.10</strong></td><td>Lead-acid — starter (piston-engine) batteries</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>8507.20</strong></td><td>Other lead-acid batteries</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>8507.30</strong></td><td>Nickel-cadmium</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>8507.50</strong></td><td>Nickel-metal hydride</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>8507.60</strong></td><td>Lithium-ion</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>8507.80</strong></td><td>Other accumulators</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>8507.90</strong></td><td>Parts</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">
        Codes follow the Harmonized System (HS); the 6-digit level is globally harmonised, with country-specific
        digits beyond it.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the code matters</h2>
      <p>
        The HS code is the key to trade data and tariffs. It is how customs counts volume and value, and how
        governments apply duties — which is why a trade dispute over "batteries" is really a dispute over specific
        codes. When a headline says "battery exports," it almost always aggregates several 8507 subheadings, so
        the code is the only way to know what is actually being measured.
      </p>

      <h2>China's position in the trade</h2>
      <p>
        China is the world's largest battery exporter across both major chemistries — lead-acid (where it ships
        hundreds of millions of units) and lithium-ion (where it holds roughly 80% of global capacity). The trade
        picture splits by chemistry: lead-acid is a high-volume, low-unit-value flow, while lithium-ion is a
        higher-value flow concentrated toward EV- and storage-manufacturing markets. See
        <a href="/china-lead-acid-industry/">China lead-acid</a> and
        <a href="/china-lithium-industry/">China lithium</a> for the output behind the trade.
      </p>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>Position:</strong> The HS code is the unsung foundation of battery market intelligence — any trade
        number without its code is a claim without a definition, and the code is what separates a lead-acid flow from
        a lithium-ion one in the statistics.</p>
        <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> Trade figures look precise but are only as precise as the code they aggregate. The
        analyst who knows 8507.10 from 8507.60 — starter lead-acid from lithium-ion — reads the data correctly and
        sees the two very different businesses that a single "battery exports" number hides.</p>
